==English==
{{was wotd|2010|June|6}}

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|xno|redunder}}, {{uder|en|frm|redonder}}, and their source, {{uder|en|la|rēdundō}}, from {{m|la|rē}} + {{m|la|undō||surge}}, from {{m|la|unda||a wave}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈdaʊnd/|/ɹəˈdaʊnd/|a=UK,US}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-redound.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|aʊnd|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To [[swell]] up (of water, waves etc.); to [[overflow]], to [[surge]] (of bodily fluids). {{defdate|14th|19th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|passage=For every dram of hony therein found / A pound of gall doth over it '''redound''' […].|4|10}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[contribute]] to an [[advantage]] or [[disadvantage]] for someone or something. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=a. 1729|author={{w|John Rogers (priest)|John Rogers}}|title=A prudent cobduct recommended and enforced
|passage=The honour done to our religion ultimately '''redounds''' to God, the author of it.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1970|author=Alvin Toffler|title=Future Shock|page=448|publisher=Bantam Books
|passage=The fact that in one case the advance '''redounds''' to private advantage and in the other, theoretically, to the public good, does not alter the core assumptions common to both.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[contribute]] to  the honour, shame etc. of a person or organisation. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Swift Gulliver|part=II|page=259|passage=I did not omit even our Sports and Paſtimes, or any other Particular which I thought might '''redound''' to the Honour of my Country.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Peter Preston|journal=The Observer|date=2 Mar 2008
|passage=One thing about the ‘John McCain-didn’t-sleep-with-a-lobbyist’ story '''redounds''' to the New York Times’ credit.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019|author=el:Αντώνιος Καλδέλλης<tr:{{w|Anthony Kaldellis}}>|title=Byzantium Unbound|series=Past Imperfect|location={{w|Leeds}}|publisher=[[d:Q63121836|Arc Humanities Press]]|isbn=978-1-64189-199-8|section=chapter 4: “Byzantium Was Not Medieval”|pages=87–88|text=Runciman viewed many of the Crusades’ protagonists with sympathy, but he thought that the movement as a whole was destructive and did not '''redound''' to the honour<!-- page break --> of the faith.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[reverberate]], to [[echo]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[reflect]] (honour, shame etc.) {{m|en|to}} or {{m|en|onto}} someone. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To attach, come back, accrue {{m|en|to}} someone; to reflect back {{m|en|on}} or {{m|en|upon}} someone (of honour, shame etc.). {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|His infamous behaviour only '''redounded''' back upon him when he was caught.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:China Miéville|title=A Spectre, Haunting: On the Communist Manifesto|year=2022|oclc=1331706453|chapter=3|passage={{...|This long sequence skilfully deploys the rhetorical tropes of ''procatalepsis'' and ''concessio'', pre-emption and concession:}} that is, they concede the accuracy of certain classic attacks on communism, but in ways that '''redound''' on their opponents.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[arise]] {{m|en|from}} or {{m|en|out of}} something. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|of a wave, flood, etc.}} To [[roll]] back; to be [[sent]] or [[driven]] back.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=7|passage=The evil, soon driven back, '''redounded''' as a flood on those from whom it sprung.}}

